          <h2>A market town on Birmingham's edge</h2>
          <p>Sutton Coldfield keeps a distinct identity from <a href="birmingham.html">Birmingham</a> proper — its own town centre, its own retail streets, and a "Royal" title that locals are quietly proud of. A good number of the breakdowns we attend here happen on the roads connecting Sutton Coldfield back to the rest of the conurbation, where traffic moves quickly and includes a mix of local residents and longer commutes. If you've broken down on one of these roads rather than in the town centre, your direction of travel and the nearest junction help us plan the safest way to reach you.</p>

          <h2>A town built around a park and a railway</h2>
          <p>Sutton Park sits right on the edge of the town — one of the largest urban parks in Europe — and the surrounding neighbourhoods, including Mere Green and Four Oaks, are largely residential. That means some of our call-outs are on quieter suburban roads rather than main routes, so a nearby landmark or road name is genuinely useful information for us.</p>

          <h2>Rail and road together</h2>
          <p>With a station on the Cross-City Line into Birmingham New Street, a number of Sutton Coldfield residents combine rail travel with driving — parking at the station or picking someone up from it. A breakdown around the station car park or on the approach roads at peak commuting times can be more disruptive than usual, so let us know if that's your situation and we'll aim to get the vehicle clear as quickly as we can.</p>

          <div class="callout">
            <p><strong>Broken down near Sutton Park or on a quiet residential road?</strong> Stay warm, keep your hazards on if it's safe to remain with the car, and give us the nearest road name, junction or landmark rather than relying on a postcode alone.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

